  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1036 PM CDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nA warm and calm night is on display as a 593 dam ridge, currently\ncentered over the ArkLaTex, continues to dominate the weather\npattern across the region. All four of our climate sites\n(Jonesboro, Memphis, Jackson, and Tupelo) broke maximum\ntemperature records Saturday with readings in the upper 90s to\nlower 100s. Sunday will be a near rinse and repeat thanks to this\nanomalous September ridge. Ridging starts to weaken slightly by\nMonday as a cut-off upper-low begins to push across the upper\nMidwest. Near record-breaking heat will still be present Monday,\nbut come Tuesday, we will finally begin to \"cool\" closer to\nnormal as an attendant cold front begins to dip across the Mid-\nSouth. Ahead of this front, isolated to scattered shower and\nthunderstorm chances will exist as forecast PWATs begin to\nincrease to around 1.7\", nearing the 75th percentile for this\ntime of the year. Though such PWAT values will likely not support\nheavy downpours, we will appreciate any rainfall after the\nprolonged dry spell we've been in. Isolated to scattered chances\nwill carry into Tuesday with appreciable rainfall not expected.\n\nFor the first time in nearly 50 days, a cooler airmass will\nfilter in behind the leading front, supporting forecast high\ntemperatures in the 80s and lows in the 60s across much of the\nMid-South by Wednesday. As far as rain chances are concerned past\nTuesday, drier air looks to filter in behind the front,\ndiminishing chances for the latter half of the workweek. \nEnsemble guidance continues to hint at this trend of cooler\ntemperatures persisting into next weekend with northwest flow\nbuilding in aloft. Looking ahead, the latest 8-10 day temperature\nand rainfall outlooks have us only slightly above normal heading\ninto October. We'll gladly welcome this after being well above\nnormal and dry for such an extended period.\n\nAEH",
